\section*{10 Lectures and 42 Open Problems — Sum of Squares approximation ratio for Max-Cut}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The two manuscripts give computational hardness results. The first claims that a deterministic polynomial-time Max-Cut approximation with any fixed factor above $\alpha_{\rm GW}$ would imply $\mathrm P=\mathrm{NP}$. The second claims the Unique Games gap theorem, which supplies hardness consequences for approximation problems. Their relevance here is an obstruction to improving deterministic polynomial-time rounding; neither manuscript identifies the unconditional degree-four sum-of-squares ratio.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)$ denote the largest weight crossing a vertex bipartition, and set
\[
 \alpha_{\rm GW}=\min_{-1\le\rho<1}\frac{2\arccos\rho}{\pi(1-\rho)}.
\]
For each fixed $\alpha\in(\alpha_{\rm GW},1]$, the claim is NP-hardness of returning a cut of weight at least $\alpha\operatorname{MaxCut}(G)$, even on simple unweighted graphs. Its more explicit weighted gap uses any rational $0<t<1$, $B(t)=2\arcsin(t)/\pi$, and $0<\varepsilon<(t-B(t))/4$: distinguishing the two promises
\[
 \operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\ge(1+t)/2-\varepsilon
 \quad\hbox{and}\quad
 \operatorname{MaxCut}(G)\le(1+B(t))/2+\varepsilon
\]
is NP-hard when the rational edge weights sum to one.

\paragraph{Manuscript 2.}
For every fixed $\varepsilon,\delta\in(0,1/2)$, the claim gives $s\ge1$ and a deterministic polynomial-time $3$SAT reduction to explicit unweighted Unique Games over $K=\mathbb F_2^s$, on a simple bipartite graph with translation constraints $a(v_e)=a(u_e)+c_e$, satisfying
\[
 \varphi\text{ satisfiable}\Rightarrow\operatorname{val}(G_{\varphi})\ge1-\varepsilon,
 \qquad\varphi\text{ unsatisfiable}\Rightarrow\operatorname{val}(G_{\varphi})\le\delta.
\]
Alphabet size and polynomial constants depend only on the two fixed errors. For any fixed constraint-language instance $J$ with $A=\operatorname{OPT}(J)<B=\operatorname{SDP}_{R}(J)$, the associated consequence claims NP-hardness of distinguishing value at least $B-\eta$ from value at most $A+\eta$ for any fixed $0<\eta<(B-A)/2$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 102; 2026-09-23), an affine game is composed directly with folded Gaussian sign tables. Excess stability reveals a hidden coordinate through restriction Fourier mass and vector-valued hashing, yielding endpoint labelers that contradict the outer game's low value.

For manuscript 2 (family 102; 2026-09-23), a latent quadratic alphabet and matrix-slice tests supply an outer permutation game. High acceptance decodes agreeing advice strategies, whereas clean-coordinate estimates from the unsatisfiable parity instance force their agreement to decay, giving the soundness contradiction.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The question includes the performance of polynomial-time rounding. A deterministic rounding rule that guaranteed a factor above $\alpha_{\rm GW}$ relative to $\operatorname{SOS}_4(G)$ would also achieve that factor relative to $\operatorname{OPT}(G)$, because $\operatorname{SOS}_4(G)\ge\operatorname{OPT}(G)$. Manuscript 1 therefore supplies a conditional obstruction to such a rule when $\mathrm P\ne\mathrm{NP}$. This conclusion concerns deterministic guarantees; it does not by itself rule out a randomized algorithm under that assumption. Neither linked theorem computes $\inf_G\operatorname{OPT}(G)/\operatorname{SOS}_4(G)$ or answers the near-bipartite rounding question. The match remains partial.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For the selected results, the Lean scope page identifies \texttt{OptimalMaxCut.lean} and \texttt{OAI.OptimalMaxCut.main}, and \texttt{UniqueGamesTheorem.lean} and \texttt{OAI.UniqueGamesTheorem.theorem11}. The other Comparator links belong to companion papers in the same family. This scope concerns the stated hardness theorems; it does not cover the unconditional degree-four integrality ratio or the near-bipartite question. The site has not run these Lean files or independently checked their proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

